1. Introduction
Parkinson’s disease is the second most common neurodegenerative disorder worldwide, with global prevalence reaching approximately 8.5 million people by 2019 and projections pointing to a continued rise as populations age [1]. The condition stems from the gradual death of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, and the troubling reality is that motor symptoms rarely appear until dopamine levels have already dropped by somewhere between 60 and 80 percent [2]. By the time a patient walks into a clinic with noticeable tremors or rigidity, a substantial portion of the relevant neural circuitry has already been lost, and the window for meaningful early intervention has largely closed.
The diagnostic challenge is compounded by the fact that Parkinson’s disease shares motor features with a range of other movement disorders, including essential tremor, atypical parkinsonism, and drug-induced conditions. Distinguishing between these groups on clinical grounds alone is genuinely difficult, particularly in the early stages when symptoms are subtle and overlapping, and getting the diagnosis wrong carries real consequences for how a patient is treated. Standard practice still relies heavily on physician assessment using the unified PD rating scale, which is a useful tool but remains inherently subjective — systematic reviews have found that even movement disorder experts achieve a clinical diagnosis accuracy of only around 80 percent at a first consultation [3]. Advanced imaging modalities such as MRI, PET, and SPECT can add useful information, but their cost and limited availability in many healthcare settings make them impractical for routine screening or continuous monitoring [4].
These constraints have motivated a growing body of work on automated PD detection using machine learning and deep learning. Voice recordings, gait signals, handwriting samples, and neuroimaging data have all been explored as diagnostic inputs, and the results in controlled settings are often impressive, with reported accuracies frequently exceeding 90% [5], [6]. However, most of these systems address a simpler formulation of the problem — they separate PD cases from healthy controls and stop there, leaving out the people with other movement disorders who are arguably the hardest group to handle. When those other conditions are brought into the picture, the classification problem becomes substantially harder, and the gap between what the papers report and what would actually work in a clinic widens considerably.
Wearable devices offer a practical path forward. Smartwatches equipped with accelerometer and gyroscope sensors can capture motor behaviour continuously and unobtrusively, without the infrastructure demands of imaging equipment or the controlled conditions required for reliable voice recording [6], [7]. The PADS dataset, released on PhysioNet in 2024, was collected with exactly this approach: participants performed standardised movement tasks while wearing smartwatches on both wrists, and the resulting recordings were collected alongside demographic and questionnaire data from healthy controls, PD patients, and individuals with other movement disorders [8].
This paper presents and compares two frameworks for three-class PD screening and decision support built on the PADS dataset. The first is a two-stage hybrid pipeline in which a multimodal deep network processes smartwatch motion signals and demographic variables jointly, extracting compact learned representations that are subsequently classified by a gradient boosting model. The second is a physics-informed handcrafted feature pipeline that derives 150 clinically motivated descriptors from each signal segment and trains an ensemble of gradient boosting classifiers directly on these features, evaluated under five-fold patient-level cross-validation. Predictions from both pipelines are evaluated at the patient level, where outputs from multiple sessions per subject are aggregated before the final label is assigned; the handcrafted pipeline is additionally evaluated at the segment level and the deep pipeline at the recording level, reflecting the different input units used by each approach.
The main contributions of this work are as follows:
•
A unified three-class formulation that evaluates Parkinson’s disease, healthy controls, and other movement disorders within one model, rather than through separate pairwise binary experiments.
•
A patient-disjoint five-fold cross-validation design with dataset-wide out-of-fold predictions for all 469 participants, enabling patient-level confusion, ROC-AUC, sensitivity, specificity, and failure-case analyses.
•
A paired patient-level statistical comparison of two complementary modelling pipelines using exact McNemar testing and paired stratified bootstrap analysis.
•
An explicit evaluation of temporally overlapping segmentation, including overlap-sensitivity analysis and patient-level aggregation, to distinguish dense within-patient sampling from independent evidence.
•
Component and feature-group ablation analyses used to simplify the evaluated models and determine whether attention, residual connections, demographic fusion, and individual handcrafted feature families contribute consistently.
•
Out-of-fold explainability and failure analysis combining global and class-specific SHAP attribution with borderline and high-confidence patient-level errors.
•
A comparative implementation of established deep-embedding and physics-informed handcrafted approaches under patient-level evaluation, without claiming novelty for the underlying CNN or gradient-boosting algorithms.
The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews related work on automated PD detection across different data modalities. Section 3 describes the dataset, preprocessing pipeline, feature extraction methods, and the proposed frameworks in detail. Section 4 outlines the experimental setup. Sections 5 Results and discussion, 6 Discussion present and discuss the results, and Section 7 concludes the paper.
2. Related work
Automated PD detection has been explored across several data modalities over the past decade. The sections below trace the main directions this research has taken and explain where the present work fits within that landscape.
2.1. Voice and speech-based approaches
Speech has long been investigated as a promising biomarker for Parkinson’s disease because hypokinetic dysarthria affects a large proportion of patients, while voice recordings are inexpensive, non-invasive, and easy to acquire in both clinical and remote settings. Recent reviews have shown that machine learning models based on acoustic biomarkers, including jitter, shimmer, pitch variation, and cepstral features, can achieve strong performance for distinguishing PD patients from healthy controls under controlled conditions [6]. In a large clinical cohort, Suppa et al. [9] analysed voice recordings from 115 patients with Parkinson’s disease and 108 healthy controls, reporting classification accuracies up to 93.5% depending on disease stage and speech task. Additional studies have similarly demonstrated that combining conventional acoustic descriptors with modern machine learning algorithms can improve sensitivity to subtle vocal impairments, reinforcing the value of speech analysis as a scalable digital biomarker for early screening and disease monitoring [10]. Collectively, these findings suggest that vocal abnormalities may be detectable even in early-stage PD, supporting speech analysis as a practical tool for progression tracking and longitudinal assessment.
2.2. Gait and wearable sensor approaches
Gait analysis emerged as a natural complement to voice, since walking irregularities are among the most visible motor symptoms of PD and wearable sensors make continuous measurement practical. Balaji et al. [11] extracted spatiotemporal features from vertical ground reaction force sensors and achieved 98.4% SVM accuracy in classifying motor severity on the Hoehn and Yahr scale. Zhao et al. [12] took a different hardware route with a smart anklet using triaxial accelerometer and gyroscope signals, and an improved KNN classifier reached 96.5% in PD grade diagnosis. Kour et al. [13] reviewed the gait detection field broadly and found that inertial sensors consistently dominate the literature with around 93% average effectiveness, while smartphone-based systems are starting to close the gap.
Ghayvat et al. [14] went further by fusing plantar pressure, strain, and IMU signals with a CNN-Transformer architecture for freezing of gait detection. The results were strong, but the system required a combination of specialised sensors that would be difficult to deploy outside a clinical or research setting. This tension between performance and practicality runs through most of the gait literature.
2.3. Neuroimaging and clinical data approaches
Neuroimaging has produced some of the highest accuracy figures in the PD detection literature. Islam et al. [15] combined 12 clinical assessment tests with 7500 MRI scans from the PPMI database, achieving 98.44% with an Extra Trees Classifier on the clinical data and 85.08% with DenseNet169 on MRI images, and used LIME and SHAP to explain the model decisions. Priyadharshini et al. [16] extracted radiomics features from T2-weighted MRI scans and found that Gradient Boosting with SMOTE reached 96.8%. Balamurugan and Sivasankari [17] pushed accuracy to 99.75% using the Segment Anything Model with Inception-ResNet-v2, though they acknowledged that 3D MRI models generalise poorly across clinical sites.
The practical problem with all of these systems is the imaging equipment itself. MRI and PET scanners are expensive, scarce in many healthcare settings, and unsuitable for the kind of continuous or repeated monitoring that would actually be useful for tracking disease progression.
2.4. Multimodal and hybrid approaches
A growing body of work has tried to combine data sources to get around the limitations of any single modality. Aggarwal et al. [18] worked with the PPMI Online dataset and used explainable AI to detect sleep disorders as a non-motor PD marker, reaching 90.6%. Shanthini and Chandrasekar [19] fused voice recordings and handwriting images through a deep learning framework optimised with the Chronological Tangent Search algorithm and reported 89.69%. Zhao et al. [20] surveyed the multimodal AI landscape for PD and identified wrist-worn motion data combined with clinical metadata as the most promising direction for large-scale screening.
On the deployment side, Cardenas et al. [21] built a smartwatch system that combined motion and speech data with an LLM-powered chatbot for continuous monitoring, which is one of the few examples of a multimodal PD system designed with real-world use in mind. Keserwani et al. [5] reviewed the broader field and noted that hybrid LSTM-CNN architectures have reached 98.80% in some configurations, but nearly all of these results come from small or single-modality datasets that may not reflect clinical reality.
2.5. Prior work on the PADS dataset
The PADS dataset was introduced by Varghese et al. [22], who also published the first machine learning results on it. Their pipeline combined classical signal processing with supervised learning under nested five-fold cross-validation, and achieved a balanced accuracy of 91.16% when separating PD patients from healthy controls and 72.42% when separating PD from other movement disorders. These were two separate experiments rather than a single three-class model, and the drop from 91% to 72% when other movement disorders were introduced already signals how much harder that boundary is to draw.
Yu et al. [23] subsequently evaluated a channel-attention LSTM–FCN on PADS, reporting accuracies of 92.15% for PD versus healthy controls, 85.38% for PD versus differential diagnoses, and 78.23% for the combined three-class task. Thus, three-class classification had already been investigated on PADS. The present study addresses a complementary methodological question by comparing physics-informed handcrafted features and learned deep embeddings under dataset-wide patient-level out-of-fold validation, paired statistical testing, and explainability-guided failure analysis.
2.6. Positioning of the present work
This work extends prior PADS studies through a patient-level comparison of two complementary three-class pipelines. The auxiliary binary experiments retained in this study are used only to characterise specific class boundaries and should not be interpreted as substitutes for the primary three-class evaluation.
The principal novelty of the present study lies in its evaluation design and validation framework rather than in the introduction of a new classification algorithm. CNN-based representation learning, XGBoost, LightGBM, and probability-level ensemble methods are well-established techniques. They are used here as complementary modelling tools to investigate a more demanding and clinically relevant evaluation setting: unified three-class screening with patient-disjoint partitioning, dataset-wide out-of-fold predictions, patient-level aggregation, fold-wise variability analysis, paired statistical comparison, overlap-sensitivity evaluation, and class-specific error analysis.
Beyond the unified three-class formulation, the study is distinguished by two validation-oriented design choices. First, we compare two fundamentally different approaches side by side: a pipeline built on 150 clinically motivated handcrafted features and a two-stage deep learning pipeline that extracts compact embeddings before classification. The comparison therefore focuses on two complementary supervised modelling strategies rather than a comprehensive survey of all contemporary wearable-signal architectures. Evaluating both pipelines on the same dataset under comparable patient-level cross-validation frameworks makes it possible to examine their relative strengths while acknowledging differences in their input units, fold assignments, and aggregation procedures. Second, all results are reported at the patient level by aggregating predictions across multiple recording sessions per subject. Individual segment or recording predictions carry more noise than a patient-level decision, and patient-level evaluation is more clinically relevant than segment-level evaluation, although it does not constitute real-world clinical validation. Accordingly, the present work is positioned as a controlled-condition methodological evaluation rather than a deployment study.
