3.1. Dataset description
This study uses the Parkinson’s Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset, which was made publicly available on PhysioNet in 2024. The dataset was collected using smart devices to capture digital biomarkers obtained through movement-based neurological assessments for Parkinson’s disease. The dataset includes recordings from Parkinson’s disease patients, healthy controls, and individuals with different types of movement disorders.
The PADS dataset contains data from 469 individuals and includes a total of 5159 measurement sessions. Participants were required to perform interactive movement tasks while wearing two smartwatches placed on both wrists and connected to a smartphone application. The smartwatches captured motion data while participants performed movement tasks designed by neurologists to identify subtle motor abnormalities associated with Parkinson’s disease.
The dataset consists of two main data modalities: movement sensor data and questionnaire data. The movement data include acceleration and rotational signals captured using smartwatch sensors. The questionnaire data include demographic information, medical history, and non-motor symptoms associated with Parkinson’s disease.
Motion signals were recorded using accelerometer and gyroscope sensors operating at a sampling rate of 100 Hz. The dataset includes multiple movement tasks such as resting, arm lifting, holding weight, drinking motion, finger pointing, and rhythmic foot movement, which were designed to provoke tremor and movement irregularities.
The dataset contains a class imbalance problem because it includes more Parkinson’s disease patients than healthy control subjects. The class distribution comprises approximately 276 PD cases, 114 other movement disorder cases, and 79 healthy controls, giving imbalance ratios that require explicit compensation during model training and evaluation.
The 114 participants assigned to the Other Movement Disorders class were derived from four diagnostic categories in the original PADS metadata: 60 participants labelled as Other Movement Disorders, 28 with Essential Tremor, 15 with Atypical Parkinsonism, and 11 with Multiple Sclerosis. These categories were combined into a single non-PD movement-disorder class for the primary three-class analysis. Free-text diagnostic comments were available for 107 of the 114 participants, while detailed comments were not reported for seven participants. Because several individual subtypes were small and the free-text descriptions were not encoded as standardised diagnostic labels, subtype-specific model training and cross-validation were not considered statistically reliable (see Table 1).

