Patient-level partitioning was performed before window assignment and model training. All windows belonging to a given participant were therefore assigned exclusively to either the training or test partition within each fold, and no recording or segment from the same subject crossed partition boundaries. Overlap introduced within-patient temporal correlation but did not create cross-partition information leakage.
3.2.1. Noise handling and inference-time processing
No dedicated denoising filter or learned artefact-removal module was applied in the reported experiments. Instead, the same deterministic preprocessing procedure was used during training and inference. Each recording was segmented independently to prevent windows from crossing task or recording boundaries. For the handcrafted pipeline, non-finite feature values produced by degenerate segments were replaced with zero, and feature standardisation was performed using the mean and standard deviation estimated from the corresponding training fold. The stored training-fold transformation was then applied unchanged to the held-out or inference data. For the deep pipeline, fixed-length six-channel motion windows were processed directly by the trained CNN-demographic network without test-time parameter adaptation.
Predictions from multiple segments or recordings belonging to the same participant were subsequently combined at the patient level. This aggregation can reduce the influence of isolated inconsistent predictions caused by transient disturbances; however, it should not be interpreted as a substitute for explicit signal-quality assessment or artefact rejection.
3.3. Feature extraction
Feature extraction was carried out along two parallel paths to represent the motion signals from complementary perspectives. The first path extracted physics-informed handcrafted descriptors using clinical knowledge of Parkinsonian motor symptoms. The second path learned abstract temporal representations through a deep neural network.
