3.3.2. Physics-informed handcrafted features
A comprehensive set of 150 features was extracted from each 512-sample segment across four clinically motivated categories: time-domain descriptors, frequency-domain descriptors, tremor-specific descriptors, and multi-scale wavelet residuals. An initial 160-feature version also included 10 cross-axis descriptors; however, these descriptors were excluded from the final model after exploratory ablation analysis showed that they did not consistently improve patient-level performance. The leave-one-group-out ablation reported in Section 5.7 was subsequently used to verify the contribution of each of the four retained feature categories under the corrected patient-level labels.
Time-domain features (60 features) captured amplitude and distributional characteristics of each sensor channel, including mean, standard deviation, root mean square, range, interquartile range, kurtosis, skewness, mean absolute difference, zero-crossing rate, and mean absolute value. These statistics characterise the overall movement intensity and variability associated with motor impairment.
Frequency-domain features (48 features) were derived from the magnitude spectrum of each channel. For each channel, the dominant frequency, spectral entropy, spectral centroid, mean frequency, and band power ratios in four bands were computed: 0–3 Hz (slow drift), 3–7 Hz (resting tremor band), 7–15 Hz (voluntary movement), and 15–30 Hz (high-frequency noise). The 3–7 Hz tremor band is clinically significant, as Parkinsonian resting tremor characteristically falls within this range and distinguishes it from essential tremor, which peaks above 8 Hz [13].
Tremor-specific features (30 features) were computed per channel and included the tremor power ratio (energy in 3–7 Hz relative to remaining spectral energy), peak frequency within the tremor band, first-order autocorrelation (movement regularity), waveform length, and number of spectral peaks within the tremor band. Multi-scale wavelet-approximation residuals (12 features) completed the set, capturing signal detail at three temporal scales (4, 8, and 16 samples) for the primary accelerometer and gyroscope axes.
