3.3.3. Deep temporal features
The deep feature extraction path relied on a multimodal network trained to classify recordings into three screening categories. The network processed two inputs simultaneously: a segmented motion signal of shape (2048×6)
 and a 7-dimensional demographic vector. The motion branch used two one-dimensional convolutional layers with 32 and 64 filters, each followed by max-pooling. Based on the ablation analysis (Section 5.7), the attention block was not retained in the final model. Instead, a CNN-demographic embedding network was used, in which convolutional motion representations were fused with a 32-dimensional demographic representation and passed through a 128-unit embedding layer. Once training was complete, the embedding layer was used to transform recordings into 128-dimensional feature vectors for downstream gradient boosting classification.
3.4. Machine learning models
Three classical machine learning algorithms were employed across the classification stages: Logistic Regression, Support Vector Machine, and Random Forest. Each model was evaluated under the same preprocessing pipeline to allow fair comparison.
Logistic Regression served as the baseline model. It was trained using the L-BFGS solver with a maximum of 1000 iterations. In Stage 0, the model operated without class balancing to establish a reference point, while in subsequent stages, balanced class weights were applied to account for the skewed label distribution between PD and non-PD cases.
The Support Vector Machine was configured with an RBF kernel, a regularisation parameter 𝐶=1.0
, and automatic kernel coefficient scaling (gamma = scale). Balanced class weights were enforced in all stages, given the clinical asymmetry between diagnostic groups. This configuration makes the decision boundary more sensitive to minority-class samples, which is particularly relevant when the cost of missing a PD case outweighs that of a false alarm.
Random Forest was trained with 300 estimators and no depth restriction, allowing trees to grow fully. Balanced class weights were applied at the tree level. For this model, feature importance scores were extracted after training and used to assess the relative contribution of each input variable to the classification decision.
Class imbalance was handled using a scenario-specific strategy. SMOTE with five nearest neighbours was applied only to the training partitions of the auxiliary Stage 0 and Stage 1 experiments, which operated on a small set of demographic and questionnaire variables. Synthetic samples were generated exclusively from the training data, without modifying the validation or test partitions. SMOTE was not applied to the raw motion signals, learned deep embeddings, or the final 150-dimensional handcrafted feature representation. In Stage 2 and in the principal motion-based pipelines, imbalance was instead addressed using model-level class weights or inverse-frequency sample weights. This choice avoided synthetic linear interpolation in the higher-dimensional, correlated, and potentially nonlinear motion-feature spaces.
Numerical features, specifically age, age at diagnosis, height, and weight, were standardised using z-score normalisation before being passed to Logistic Regression and SVM, as both models are sensitive to feature scale. Although Random Forest does not require scaling, the same transformation was applied for pipeline consistency.
In the Stage 2 experiments, five additional features were constructed from the original variables: disease duration (computed as the difference between current age and age at diagnosis), a BMI proxy, the weight-to-height ratio, and two interaction terms combining age with weight and BMI. These engineered features were intended to capture composite clinical signals that individual variables may not reflect on their own. LightGBM was also included in Stage 2 alongside Logistic Regression to assess whether a gradient boosting approach could better exploit these derived features. It was configured with 300 estimators, a learning rate of 0.05, and 31 leaf nodes per tree.
3.5. Handcrafted feature pipeline
To assess whether domain-informed features could match or exceed the performance of learned embeddings, a dedicated classification pipeline was constructed using the 150 handcrafted features described in Section 3.3.2. Two gradient boosting classifiers were evaluated: XGBoost, configured with 500 estimators, maximum tree depth of 6, learning rate of 0.04, subsampling rates of 0.8 for both rows and columns, minimum child weight of 3, 𝛾=0.1
, and L1 and L2 regularisation coefficients of 0.1 and 1.0 respectively; and LightGBM, configured with 500 estimators, maximum depth of 6, learning rate of 0.04, subsampling rates of 0.8, minimum child samples of 15, and regularisation coefficients of 0.1 for both L1 and L2.
Class imbalance was addressed by computing sample weights proportional to the inverse class frequency and passing them during training. A soft-voting ensemble combining XGBoost at 60% weight and LightGBM at 40% was also evaluated. The 60:40 ratio was retained as a fixed development-stage heuristic and was kept unchanged across all patient-level cross-validation folds; it was not optimised using the outer-test predictions. Formally, the ensemble probability vector for segment 𝑚
