To obtain patient-level performance estimates across unseen subject subsets, all models were evaluated under a five-fold cross-validation protocol applied at the patient level. At each fold, patients were partitioned into training and test sets using stratified sampling to preserve class proportions, ensuring that no recordings from the same subject appeared in both partitions. The mean and standard deviation of segment-level and patient-level accuracy and macro F1-score were computed across the five folds, and dataset-wide out-of-fold predictions across all 469 subjects were used for the class-wise, confusion-matrix, and ROC-AUC analyses reported in Section 5.
3.6. Deep learning models
The deep learning component of the proposed framework was developed through a series of progressively refined architectures, moving from a single-modality recurrent model towards a multimodal network with attention mechanisms and residual connections.
3.6.1. LSTM baseline
The first architecture was a straightforward LSTM network that received motion signals of shape (2048×6)
 as input. A single LSTM layer with 64 units processed the temporal sequence, followed by a dense layer with 32 units and a three-class softmax output. To account for the unequal class distribution, sample weights were computed from the training labels and passed during training. The model was trained for 10 epochs with a batch size of 32 using the Adam optimiser and sparse categorical cross-entropy loss. This configuration served as the deep learning baseline, establishing the performance achievable from motion signals alone without any spatial feature extraction prior to recurrent processing.
3.6.2. CNN-LSTM
The second architecture replaced the direct LSTM input with a convolutional front-end to extract local motion patterns before temporal modelling. Two convolutional layers with 32 and 64 filters and a kernel size of 5 were applied in sequence, each followed by max-pooling with a pool size of 2. The compressed sequence was then passed to an LSTM layer with 64 units. A dense layer with 32 units preceded the softmax output. This design separates the role of local feature extraction from sequential dependency modelling, allowing the LSTM to operate on a more compact and structured representation of the signal.
3.6.3. Multimodal attention network
The third architecture introduced two changes: a demographic input branch and a multi-head attention mechanism. The motion branch followed the same two-layer convolutional structure used in the CNN-LSTM model. Instead of feeding the convolutional output into an LSTM, a Multi-Head Attention layer with 4 heads and key dimension 32 was applied to capture long-range temporal dependencies within the sequence, followed by Layer Normalisation and Global Average Pooling. A separate branch processed the 7-dimensional demographic vector through a 16-unit dense layer. Both branches were concatenated and passed through a 32-unit fusion layer before the output.
3.6.4. Final CNN-demographic embedding network
Before selecting the final architecture, an attention-based residual model was also evaluated as a candidate. This candidate architecture extended the multimodal attention network with residual connections, dropout regularisation, and an expanded embedding layer, following the pattern of transformer-style blocks: after the two convolutional layers, the attention output was added back to the convolutional features through a residual connection before Layer Normalisation, dropout with a rate of 0.3 was applied after normalisation, and the demographic branch was expanded to 32 units with a dropout rate of 0.2. It was then subjected, together with the two preceding attention-based designs, to a component-level ablation study (Section 5.7) that individually removed the residual connection, the attention block, and the demographic branch to quantify each component’s contribution under five-fold patient-level cross-validation.
The final deep architecture retained for the proposed two-stage framework omits both the attention block and the residual refinement connection, neither of which provided a consistent patient-level improvement in the ablation analysis. The motion branch consists of two one-dimensional convolutional layers with 32 and 64 filters, each followed by max-pooling, with no attention or residual connection applied to their output. The demographic branch projects the 7-dimensional demographic vector into a 32-dimensional representation. The motion and demographic representations are concatenated and passed through a 128-unit embedding layer, designated as the final fusion layer.
The final CNN-demographic network was trained separately within each outer cross-validation fold using the Adam optimiser, sparse categorical cross-entropy loss, a batch size of 32, and inverse-frequency class weighting. During training, 10% of the outer-training recordings were reserved as an internal validation subset using the Keras validation-split procedure. Early stopping monitored the validation loss with a patience of three epochs and restored the best-performing model weights. The resulting network was subsequently used to extract 128-dimensional embeddings from the outer-training and untouched outer-test recordings. Recording-level probabilities were combined at the patient level using confidence-weighted aggregation, without class-specific threshold adjustment.
Once trained, the classification head was removed and the embedding layer was used as a feature extractor. This embedding layer was used to transform each recording into a 128-dimensional feature vector for downstream gradient boosting classification, forming the core of the proposed two-stage framework described in Section 3.7.
3.6.5. Second-stage classifiers
Three gradient boosting classifiers were evaluated on the extracted embeddings: XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost. XGBoost was configured with 800 estimators, a maximum depth of 8, a learning rate of 0.03, and subsampling rates of 0.85 for both rows and columns. LightGBM was trained with 1000 estimators, 64 leaves, and a learning rate of 0.03. CatBoost used 800 iterations with depth 8 and the same learning rate. In addition to individual model evaluation, a soft voting ensemble was constructed by averaging the predicted probabilities of all three classifiers and selecting the class with the highest mean probability.
For the final deep pipeline, the second-stage XGBoost probabilities were post-calibrated within each outer training fold using isotonic regression implemented through CalibratedClassifierCV with three internal folds. Calibration was performed exclusively on the outer-training embeddings, and the resulting calibrated classifier was then applied to the untouched outer-test embeddings. The outer-test patients were not used during calibration. This procedure was used to transform the classifier scores into smoother probability estimates for aggregation; however, formal calibration quality was not evaluated using Brier score, expected calibration error, calibration slope, or reliability diagrams. Therefore, the predicted probabilities should not be interpreted as clinically validated risk estimates.
3.7. Proposed two-stage hybrid framework
The proposed framework decomposes the screening problem into two sequential stages. In the first stage, the CNN-demographic embedding network described above extracts a 128-dimensional embedding from each recording. In the second stage, a gradient boosting classifier operates on these embeddings to produce the final three-class screening label. Formally,
