The total contribution of a task or session to the deep patient-level prediction was the sum of the normalised confidence weights of its associated recordings. Thus, tasks or sessions containing more recordings could contribute more cumulative evidence, while recordings with higher predicted confidence received greater influence. Task identity itself was not supplied as an explicit weighting variable, and no task-specific thresholds, class-specific aggregation weights, or manually selected clinical priorities were applied.
Because the overlapping windows used in the handcrafted pipeline are temporally correlated, the resulting segment-level predictions were not interpreted as statistically independent observations. Their aggregation was regarded as dense within-patient sampling rather than repeated independent evidence. The influence of overlap was quantified through the sensitivity analysis described in Section 3.2.
During inference, the same preprocessing, feature construction, probability estimation, and patient-level aggregation procedures used during evaluation were applied without refitting or recalibrating the models on the incoming participant data.
3.10. Evaluation metrics
Classification performance was assessed using accuracy, precision, recall, macro F1-score, balanced accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity. For the principal three-class patient-level experiments, one-vs-rest receiver operating characteristic area under the curve (ROC-AUC) was calculated separately for healthy controls, Parkinson’s disease, and Other Movement Disorders using the out-of-fold predicted probabilities. Macro-average and weighted-average ROC-AUC values were also reported. The macro-average AUC was emphasised because it assigns equal importance to all three classes and is therefore less influenced by the imbalanced class distribution. For the binary classification experiments, conventional binary ROC-AUC was used where applicable. All cross-validation results are reported as mean ±
 standard deviation across folds.
Ninety-five percent confidence intervals for the principal patient-level metrics were estimated using 10,000 stratified bootstrap resamples of the dataset-wide out-of-fold patient predictions. Patients, rather than recordings or signal segments, were used as the resampling unit. Because both pipelines generated predictions for the same 469 participants, paired statistical comparisons were performed. Accuracy was compared using an exact McNemar test based on paired correctness outcomes. Differences in macro F1-score and macro ROC-AUC were evaluated using paired stratified bootstrap resampling, with two-sided 95% confidence intervals and empirical p-values. Statistical significance was assessed at 𝛼=0.05
.
3.11. SHAP-based explainability and patient-level failure analysis
To extend model interpretation beyond conventional feature-importance ranking, an out-of-fold TreeSHAP analysis was conducted on the XGBoost component of the final XGBoost–LightGBM soft-voting ensemble. Within each of the five patient-level cross-validation folds, the XGBoost model was trained exclusively on the outer-training patients, and SHAP values were calculated only for segments belonging to the corresponding held-out outer-test patients. Consequently, all reported SHAP explanations were generated for patients not observed during model training.
The SHAP analysis was restricted to the XGBoost component because the final classifier combined two independently trained gradient-boosting models through weighted probability averaging rather than through a single differentiable decision function. The final patient-level predictions and failure cases were obtained from the complete soft-voting ensemble, using 60% XGBoost and 40% LightGBM probabilities, whereas the SHAP values describe the feature contributions within the XGBoost component of that ensemble.
Because each participant contributed multiple temporally correlated segments, segment-level SHAP values were not treated as independent observations. For local patient-level explanations, signed SHAP values were averaged across all segments belonging to the same patient. For global feature importance, the absolute SHAP magnitudes were first averaged across the segments of each patient and were subsequently averaged across all patients. This two-stage aggregation ensured that every participant contributed equally to the global interpretation, irrespective of the number of available segments.
Global SHAP importance was calculated across the three output classes, and class-specific SHAP importance was calculated separately for Healthy Controls, Parkinson’s disease, and Other Movement Disorders. Patient-level failure cases were identified from the dataset-wide out-of-fold ensemble predictions. Misclassified cases were then examined using their predicted class probabilities, confidence margins, and the highest-magnitude SHAP contributions towards the predicted class. Both borderline errors and high-confidence errors were selected to distinguish uncertainty near the class boundary from cases showing strongly misleading motion patterns.
The authoritative patient-level labels, probabilities, and failure cases were taken from the saved dataset-wide out-of-fold predictions of the final ensemble, while SHAP values were recomputed from the corrected 74,102-segment cache using the same patient-level folds.
4. Experimental setup
All experiments were implemented in Python 3.12.7. The deep learning models were built and trained using TensorFlow with the Keras API. Gradient boosting classifiers were implemented using the XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost libraries. Classical machine learning models were taken from Scikit-learn, which also provided the preprocessing and evaluation utilities. The imbalanced-learn library was used only for the SMOTE-based auxiliary demographic experiments in Stages 0 and 1; the principal motion-based pipelines relied on class or sample weighting rather than synthetic oversampling.
The principal handcrafted and final deep pipelines were evaluated using separate five-fold stratified patient-level cross-validation assignments, both with a fixed random seed of 42. For the handcrafted pipeline, StratifiedKFold was applied to the list of unique patients, and the resulting patient assignments were propagated to all corresponding segments. For the final deep pipeline, patient-level fold assignments were generated using StratifiedGroupKFold with patient identity as the grouping variable and were stored for consistent reuse. In both pipelines, all recordings and derived samples from a participant remained within a single fold, preventing patient overlap between training and test partitions. The exact patient assignments to individual folds were generated separately and were not identical between the two principal pipelines. Earlier exploratory demographic, binary, hybrid, and developmental deep-learning experiments used fixed patient-level train/test splits and are reported only as auxiliary analyses. Earlier exploratory motion-based experiments used either GroupShuffleSplit with participant identity as the grouping variable or stratified splitting of the unique patient-level labels. GroupShuffleSplit ensured patient-disjoint partitions but did not itself guarantee class stratification. When stratification was required, class proportions were preserved at the patient level, and the resulting assignments were propagated to all recordings and segments belonging to each participant. For the deep pipeline, five-fold StratifiedGroupKFold cross-validation was applied using patient identity as the grouping variable, ensuring that all recordings from the same participant remained within a single outer fold. Within each outer-training fold, 10% of the recording-level samples were used internally for validation. Early stopping monitored validation loss, and the best model weights were restored before embedding extraction and evaluation on the untouched outer-test recordings.
A random seed of 42 was fixed across all experiments to improve reproducibility. In the auxiliary Stage 0 and Stage 1 demographic experiments, SMOTE with five nearest neighbours was applied exclusively to the training partition. SMOTE was not used in Stage 2, the final handcrafted pipeline, or the final deep pipeline. These experiments instead used model-level class weighting or inverse-frequency sample weighting to preserve the observed structure of the original motion-derived samples and avoid potentially implausible synthetic interpolation in correlated and nonlinear feature spaces. No external datasets were used for pretraining or transfer learning.
Wall-clock training duration was not recorded systematically during the original cross-validation runs and is therefore not reported. Because training time is strongly hardware- and implementation-dependent, the study reports the full training protocol, model complexity, and held-out inference latency instead.
For the ablation experiments, both the deep and handcrafted pipelines were evaluated using five-fold patient-level cross-validation. For the deep component-ablation experiments, StratifiedGroupKFold was used with patient identity as the grouping variable. The same fixed deep-pipeline fold assignment was retained across all deep ablation variants. The same training settings, class weighting, embedding extraction, XGBoost classifier, and patient-level probability aggregation were retained across ablation variants, so that differences in performance could be attributed to the specific architectural or feature-group change being tested rather than to protocol variation.
TreeSHAP explanations were generated using the SHAP library. All explanations were computed out-of-fold using the same fixed patient-level folds, random seed, preprocessing procedure, class weighting, model parameters, ensemble weights, and patient-level probability aggregation used in the final handcrafted evaluation. SHAP analysis used the corrected handcrafted feature cache containing 74,102 segments generated by independent segmentation of each recording with a 512-sample window and a 128-sample step.
4.1. Computational complexity and latency evaluation
Computational complexity and inference latency were evaluated for the two principal pipelines using held-out samples from the five-fold patient-level cross-validation procedure. All measurements were performed on the same workstation and excluded model-training time. Inference was measured using batch size 1 after warm-up executions, and repeated measurements were summarised using the mean, standard deviation, median, and 95th percentile.
Computational profiling was performed on a 64-bit Windows 11 workstation equipped with a 13th-generation Intel Core i7-13620H processor operating at 2.40 GHz, 16 GB of RAM, and integrated Intel UHD Graphics. TensorFlow did not detect a compatible GPU device; therefore, all deep-learning latency measurements were obtained using CPU inference. The software environment consisted of Python 3.12.7, TensorFlow 2.16.1, XGBoost 3.1.2, LightGBM 4.6.0, Scikit-learn 1.3.2, NumPy 1.26.4, and pandas 2.3.3. The operating system exposed 16 logical CPU cores.
For the deep pipeline, 20 held-out recordings per fold were used for warm-up and excluded from timing. Latency was subsequently measured on up to 300 held-out recordings per fold, using batch size 1 and three repeated executions per recording. For the handcrafted pipeline, classifier-only latency was measured on up to 1000 held-out feature vectors per fold. End-to-end latency, including segmentation, extraction of the 150 handcrafted features, standardisation, ensemble inference, and probability aggregation, was measured on up to 40 held-out raw recordings per fold. Each handcrafted measurement was repeated three times. For each sample, the median of the repeated executions was retained before calculating the overall mean, standard deviation, median, and 95th percentile.
For the handcrafted pipeline, computational profiling included segmentation, extraction of the 150 handcrafted features, feature standardisation, XGBoost–LightGBM soft-voting inference, and probability aggregation. Classifier-only latency was also measured separately using precomputed feature vectors. For the deep pipeline, profiling included CNN-demographic embedding extraction, isotonic-calibrated XGBoost inference, and patient-level probability aggregation. Model complexity was reported using the number of neural-network parameters or decision trees, together with the serialised model size.
Because the measurements were obtained on a workstation rather than directly on a smartwatch or embedded processor, they should be interpreted as comparative implementation-level latency estimates rather than evidence of on-device real-time performance.
5. Results and discussion
5.1. Baseline classification using demographic data
The first set of experiments used only demographic and questionnaire variables, with no motion data involved. The 469 subjects were split into training and test sets at an 80/20 ratio, leaving 94 samples for evaluation. The class distribution in the training set was 221 PD cases, 91 other movement disorders, and 63 healthy controls, reflecting a notable imbalance that shaped the choice of preprocessing strategy for each model (see Table 2).
The unbalanced Logistic Regression model recorded the highest accuracy at 70%, but that number is largely driven by how well it handled the PD class, where recall reached 96%. On healthy controls it dropped to 44%, and on other movement disorders to just 26%. The model was essentially defaulting to PD for most inputs.

Table 2. Three-class classification results on demographic data (Stage 0).

Model	Accuracy	Macro F1	Weighted F1
LR (no SMOTE)	0.70	0.58	0.66
LR + SMOTE	0.62	0.63	0.63
SVM + SMOTE	0.61	0.55	0.60
Random Forest + SMOTE	0.63	0.57	0.63
Introducing SMOTE pushed the training distribution to 221 samples per class and changed the picture considerably. Recall on healthy controls jumped to 94% under the same Logistic Regression setup, though overall accuracy fell to 62% as the model became more willing to assign non-PD labels. SVM and Random Forest showed a similar shift: more balanced per-class performance, but no model exceeded 63% in overall accuracy.
Feature importance scores from the Random Forest pointed to age at diagnosis (28.3%) and current age (21.9%) as the two variables doing most of the work, with weight (20.1%) and height (17.8%) following closely. Gender, family history, and handedness contributed far less. However, the concentration of importance in age, age at diagnosis, weight, and height may reflect both clinically relevant associations and demographic differences between the diagnostic groups. Therefore, these importance scores should be interpreted as cohort-specific predictive associations rather than evidence of demographic neutrality or causal relevance.
5.2. Binary classification: PD versus non-PD
This binary analysis was performed as an auxiliary experiment to examine whether merging the two non-PD groups simplified the decision boundary. It does not represent the primary evaluation task of the study.
Stage 1 reframed the problem as a binary task by merging healthy controls and other movement disorder cases into a single non-PD group, leaving 276 PD cases against 193 non-PD cases. After the 80/20 split, the test set contained 55 PD and 39 non-PD samples. SMOTE brought the training distribution to 221 samples per class before model training (see Table 3).
Random Forest was the strongest model in this stage, reaching 70% accuracy with an F1-score of 0.75 on the PD class and 0.62 on non-PD. It correctly identified 43 of the 55 PD cases and 23 of the 39 non-PD cases. SVM came in second at 62%, with more balanced per-class performance but lower overall discrimination. Logistic Regression trailed at 59%, struggling particularly with the non-PD class where recall sat at 49%.

Table 3. Binary classification results: PD vs Non-PD (Stage 1).

Model	Accuracy	Macro F1	Weighted F1
LR + SMOTE	0.59	0.57	0.58
SVM + SMOTE	0.62	0.61	0.62
Random Forest + SMOTE	0.70	0.69	0.70
Feature importance from the Random Forest showed a more even spread than in Stage 0, with weight (22.7%), age at diagnosis (22.7%), age (22.2%), and height (19.5%) each carrying comparable weight. This suggests that in the binary setting, the four continuous variables collectively define the decision boundary rather than any single variable dominating.
Overall, the results across Stage 0 and Stage 1 point to a consistent ceiling when using demographic data alone. No model exceeded 70% accuracy, and minority class recall remained difficult to sustain without sacrificing performance elsewhere. This motivates the introduction of motion-based features in the subsequent stages.
5.3. Binary classification: PD versus other movement disorders
This pairwise experiment was included to isolate the clinically difficult boundary between Parkinson’s disease and other movement disorders and to help interpret the errors observed in the unified three-class model.
Stage 2 kept only the PD and other movement disorder cases, dropping healthy controls entirely. The resulting dataset had 276 PD cases against 114 other disorder cases, and after splitting, the test set held 55 PD and 23 other samples. Class imbalance in this experiment was handled through model-level class weights rather than SMOTE. Although the imbalance was less extreme than in the preceding experiments, the primary reason for avoiding SMOTE was methodological: linear interpolation between samples may generate synthetic combinations that do not preserve the correlated, nonlinear, and clinically constrained relationships among the demographic and engineered medical variables. Class weighting therefore allowed the minority class to receive greater influence during training without modifying the empirical feature distribution.
Five configurations were tested: three standard classifiers, Logistic Regression with engineered features, and LightGBM with the same feature set (see Table 4).
Logistic Regression posted the highest accuracy at 77%, but the confusion matrix tells a more complicated story: it caught 53 of 55 PD cases yet only 7 of 23 other disorder cases. Recall on the minority class sat at 30%, which is too low to be clinically useful on its own.

