LightGBM with the engineered features came closest to a workable balance. At 76% accuracy it identified 11 of the 23 other disorder cases while keeping PD recall at 87%, giving the highest macro F1 of the stage at 0.69. The added features, particularly disease duration and the BMI proxy, seem to have given the model something extra to separate the two groups.
Random Forest put over 90% of its importance weight on the four continuous variables: weight (24.1%), age at diagnosis (23.8%), age (22.7%), and height (20.6%). The categorical variables barely registered, which lines up with what was seen in the earlier stages.
Taking the three stages together, the demographic features topped out at 77% accuracy in the easiest binary setting and never produced reliable minority class recall. That ceiling is what the motion signals are meant to break through in the next stage.
5.4. Classification on hybrid motion and demographic features
This set of experiments used the hybrid dataset, which combined movement-derived statistical features extracted from the smartwatch recordings with the demographic variables used in the previous stages. Six configurations were evaluated, covering Random Forest, SVM, LightGBM with and without hyperparameter tuning, PCA-based dimensionality reduction paired with Random Forest, and LightGBM trained on the extended feature set that included FFT descriptors (see Table 5).
LightGBM was the clear standout in this stage. Both the base configuration and the grid-searched version reached 67% accuracy with a macro F1 of 0.62. The grid search explored combinations of tree depth, number of leaves, learning rate, and estimator count through five-fold cross-validation, but did not improve over the default LightGBM setup, suggesting the base configuration was already near the optimum for this feature set.

