Adding FFT features to the LightGBM pipeline produced a slightly lower result at 62%, which was unexpected. The FFT descriptors added 24 frequency-domain features per recording, but the increase in dimensionality appears to have introduced noise that offset any additional signal the spectral features carried.
Compared to the demographic-only experiments, the hybrid dataset did not consistently improve performance on the three-class problem. LightGBM matched the best accuracy seen in Stage 0 at 67%, while Random Forest and SVM actually performed worse than their demographic-only counterparts. This points to a mismatch between the hand-crafted statistical motion features and what the classifiers need to separate three clinically distinct groups, and motivates the shift to learned representations in the deep learning stage.
5.5. Deep learning models
Four deep learning architectures were trained and evaluated on the motion and demographic data. The test set contained 564 recordings drawn from subjects not seen during training (see Table 6).
The standalone LSTM reached 52% accuracy but produced heavily skewed predictions, concentrating most of its output on the PD class with a recall of 72% there and only 4% on healthy controls. The CNN-LSTM dropped to 27% overall, its convolutional front-end pushing nearly all predictions towards the healthy class with a recall of 92% for that group but 15% for PD.

