To determine whether the poor CNN-LSTM result was caused primarily by insufficient training duration or random initialisation, the same architecture was retrained for up to 50 epochs using early stopping and learning-rate reduction across three random seeds. The model remained consistently poor across runs, achieving a mean accuracy of 24.23%±0.80%
 and a macro F1-score of 0.233 ± 0.013. The best validation loss occurred at epochs 2–3, and training stopped after 9–10 epochs in all runs. Parkinson’s disease recall remained extremely low at 4.71%±1.27%
, while healthy-control recall remained high at 79.94%±7.34%
, indicating reproducible class-biased convergence rather than random seed instability. These findings suggest that insufficient training epochs were not the primary cause of failure. Instead, the results are more consistent with an architecture–data or training-configuration mismatch under the current 2048-sample input representation and class-imbalance setting: the CNN-LSTM exhibited consistently poor and class-biased convergence across seeds, rather than run-to-run instability.
Because the same held-out set was used for validation monitoring in this diagnostic experiment, these values are not presented as an unbiased final performance estimate, but only as a multi-seed convergence analysis.
Adding a demographic branch and replacing the LSTM with global average pooling after the attention layer lifted accuracy to 65% in both multimodal configurations. The difference between them was in how that accuracy was distributed. The first attention model achieved 98% recall on healthy controls but only 49% on other movement disorders, while the residual attention model with dropout produced a far more balanced result: 61% recall on PD, 49% on other disorders, and 98% on healthy controls, with a macro F1 of 0.63 compared to 0.38 for the earlier version.
These four architectures represent the developmental progression that led to the multimodal design, and the results above are reported on a single recording-level train/test split, as in the earlier stages. However, this comparison alone does not establish which individual component, the attention block, the residual connection, or the demographic branch, is responsible for the observed balance. Section 5.7 reports a dedicated five-fold patient-level ablation study that isolates each component and shows that the demographic branch, not the attention block, is the dominant contributor; the attention block was consequently removed from the final selected deep model described in Section 3.7.
5.6. Two-stage framework results
The 128-dimensional embeddings from the CNN-demographic embedding network were passed to three gradient boosting classifiers trained independently on the same training split. Results are reported at both the recording level and the patient level (see Table 7), where recording-level probability vectors were combined using confidence-weighted aggregation and normalised before the final label was assigned. As detailed in Section 5.7, this embedding network retains the convolutional and demographic branches but omits both the residual refinement connection and the attention block, neither of which contributed a consistent patient-level benefit in the ablation study (see Table 10).
LightGBM and the soft voting ensemble both reached 73% accuracy at the recording level, with LightGBM posting the highest weighted F1 at 0.72. XGBoost followed at 72% with comparable metrics, while CatBoost trailed slightly at 71%. Across all three classifiers the pattern was consistent: strong performance on the PD class with recall between 86% and 87%, reasonable results on healthy controls between 66% and 71%, and noticeably weaker discrimination of other movement disorders where recall ranged from 26% to 28%.

