5.8.3. Overlap-sensitivity analysis
To examine whether repeated overlapping segments artificially inflated patient-level consistency, the handcrafted pipeline was re-evaluated using the same 512-sample window length with step sizes of 128, 256, and 512 samples, corresponding to 75%, 50%, and 0% overlap, respectively. Each recording was segmented independently, and identical patient-level folds, features, model hyperparameters, class-weighting procedures, ensemble weights, and patient-level probability aggregation were retained across all three settings. Therefore, the only experimental factor changed was the segmentation overlap (see Table 15).
Removing overlap reduced patient-level accuracy from 0.7228 to 0.7058, a difference of 1.70 percentage points, while patient-level macro F1 decreased from 0.6623 to 0.6284. Therefore, dense overlapping segmentation provided a measurable aggregation benefit, particularly for class-balanced performance, but the patient-level result did not collapse when overlap was removed. Segment-level accuracy was slightly higher without overlap, increasing from 0.5362 to 0.5477, which indicates that the improvement at the patient level arose mainly from denser within-patient probability aggregation rather than from stronger individual segment classification. Because overlapping segments are temporally correlated, they were not treated as independent observations, and all train–test partitioning and final evaluation were performed at the patient level.

