5.8.5. Patient-level failure-case analysis
The dataset-wide out-of-fold ensemble predictions contained 130 misclassified patients among the 469 participants, corresponding to an error rate of 27.7%. The most frequent error pattern was Other Movement Disorders being classified as Parkinson’s disease, which occurred in 52 patients. This was followed by Parkinson’s disease being classified as Other Movement Disorders in 26 patients, Healthy Controls being classified as Parkinson’s disease in 21 patients, Parkinson’s disease being classified as Healthy in 15 patients, Other Movement Disorders being classified as Healthy in 15 patients, and Healthy Controls being classified as Other Movement Disorders in one patient.
Several errors occurred close to the patient-level decision boundary. Patient 127, whose true class was Parkinson’s disease, was classified as Other Movement Disorders with a confidence margin of only 0.0003. Patient 77, whose true class was Other Movement Disorders, was classified as Parkinson’s disease with a margin of 0.0018. Patient 246, whose true class was Parkinson’s disease, was classified as Other Movement Disorders with a margin of 0.0021. These cases represent borderline decisions in which small probability changes could alter the assigned class.
Other errors were substantially more confident. Patient 326, whose true class was Other Movement Disorders, was classified as Parkinson’s disease with a confidence margin of 0.4747. The leading XGBoost attributions towards the Parkinson’s disease output were fd_ch4_b1, fd_ch2_b1, fd_ch0_b1, fd_ch3_b1, and fd_ch5_b1. Patient 209 showed a similar high-confidence Other Movement Disorders-to-Parkinson’s disease error, driven primarily by fd_ch0_b1, fd_ch4_b1, fd_ch2_b1, fd_ch3_b0, and fd_ch3_b1. These cases indicate that some patients with alternative movement disorders exhibited strongly Parkinson-like spectral patterns within the extracted feature space.
Collectively, the failure analysis indicates two distinct error mechanisms. Some errors reflected low-confidence overlap near the class boundary, whereas others reflected high-confidence but misleading combinations of spectral and tremor-related features. The predominance of Other Movement Disorders-to-Parkinson’s disease errors is consistent with the confusion matrix, pairwise ROC-AUC results, and class-specific SHAP overlap (see Fig. 5 and Table 16).
