5.9. Comparison across pipelines
Table 17 provides a contextual summary of prior studies conducted on the PADS dataset. The numerical results should not be interpreted as a direct performance ranking because the studies differ substantially in task formulation, number of classes, evaluation unit, partitioning strategy, and reported metric. Prior work includes both binary and three-class classification tasks, whereas the present study compares two three-class pipelines using dataset-wide out-of-fold patient-level predictions obtained by aggregating multiple recording- or segment-level outputs per subject. The table is therefore included only to position the present work methodologically within the existing PADS literature. Because of these protocol differences, no direct conclusion regarding superiority or inferiority can be drawn from the absolute values alone.
The threshold-independent analysis (Table 18) showed that the handcrafted ensemble achieved higher macro-average and weighted-average ROC-AUC than the final deep No-attention pipeline. The deep pipeline achieved slightly higher discrimination for healthy controls, with an AUC of 0.927 compared with 0.918 for the handcrafted ensemble. However, the handcrafted pipeline achieved higher AUC for Parkinson’s disease and higher AUC for Other Movement Disorders. In particular, the Other Movement Disorders AUC was 0.746 in the handcrafted pipeline compared with 0.695 in the deep pipeline, indicating somewhat weaker separation of this clinically confusable group by the learned embeddings. These results confirm that the difference between the pipelines is not limited to the selected classification threshold or argmax decision rule. The handcrafted model’s one-vs-rest ROC curves are shown in Fig. 8.

