5.10. Computational complexity and inference latency
The deep No-attention pipeline contained 24,483 neural-network parameters and had a combined serialised model size of 5.02 MB, including the isotonic-calibrated second-stage classifier. Mean embedding extraction time was 15.18 ± 1.72 ms per recording, while second-stage classifier inference required 5.38 ± 0.69 ms. The resulting total recording-level latency was 20.59 ± 2.36 ms, with a median of 20.22 ms and a 95th percentile of 25.33 ms.
The handcrafted ensemble consisted of 500 XGBoost trees and 500 LightGBM trees, with a combined model size of 4.49 MB. Classifier-only inference required 2.44 ± 0.43 ms per segment. However, explicit extraction of the 150 handcrafted features required a mean of 83.80 ms per recording, resulting in an end-to-end recording latency of 87.98 ± 38.67 ms. The median latency was 69.11 ms and the 95th percentile was 152.66 ms.
Patient-level probability aggregation was negligible for both pipelines, requiring approximately 0.015 ms for the handcrafted pipeline and 0.024 ms for the deep pipeline. Overall, the deep pipeline was approximately 4.3 times faster per recording in the present workstation implementation, primarily because the handcrafted pipeline incurred a larger explicit feature-extraction cost. Nevertheless, these measurements do not establish direct smartwatch feasibility because no embedded-device or energy-consumption evaluation was conducted (see Table 21).

