6. Discussion
6.1. The limits of demographic features
Demographic and questionnaire variables were initially expected to provide stronger discriminatory information than was observed experimentally. Age at diagnosis, current age, weight, and height dominated the feature importance scores at every stage, and the categorical variables, handedness, family history, gender, contributed almost nothing. These four continuous variables appear to capture a population-level profile of who develops Parkinson’s disease, rather than the specific presentation of an individual on a given day. That distinction matters considerably for a three-class problem in which two of the classes share similar demographic profiles.
Recall for the Other Movement Disorders class remained a consistent limitation. In the demographic-only experiments it fell as low as 17% with Random Forest, meaning the model was writing off nearly the entire group. SMOTE helped with healthy controls but did not fix the other disorders class, and adding engineered features gave LightGBM a modest lift without changing the fundamental picture. The questionnaire data do not appear to contain the information required to draw that boundary.
The repeated dominance of age, age at diagnosis, weight, and height also raises the possibility of cohort-related demographic bias. These variables may capture differences in the demographic composition of the diagnostic groups rather than disease-specific motor characteristics. Consequently, part of the observed predictive performance may reflect the structure of the PADS cohort, including unequal age or body-size distributions across classes, rather than features that would generalise consistently to external populations.
Feature importance alone cannot determine whether these variables represent clinically valid risk information or act as proxies for diagnostic class membership. Their strong predictive contribution should therefore not be interpreted as evidence that demographic dependence is clinically desirable or demographically equitable. The present findings indicate predictive association within this cohort only.
6.2. What the motion data added
The introduction of learned motion embeddings improved performance relative to the earlier baselines. The earlier motion experiments with statistical features and FFT descriptors did not consistently outperform the demographic baseline, and in the FFT case actually performed worse. The problem with those features is that they summarise the signal into a fixed set of statistics and lose the temporal structure that carries most of the diagnostic information in a tremor or movement task.
The multimodal architectures handled that differently from the earlier single-modality models. By processing the full 2048-step sequence through convolutional layers and fusing it with demographic information, the network could pick up local motion patterns while retaining population-level context that the motion signal alone does not carry, and the best of these configurations reached a macro F1 of 0.63 at the recording level.
However, the ablation analysis in Section 5.7 showed that demographic fusion contributed more consistently than the attention block in the deep pipeline: removing the demographic branch collapsed macro F1 from 0.582 to 0.247, while removing the attention block left patient-level accuracy essentially unchanged and slightly reduced fold-to-fold variability. This suggests that, for the current dataset size and patient-level setting, compact convolutional representations combined with demographic information were more stable than a heavier attention-based encoder, and it is why the attention block was not retained in the final deep model.
The present experiments do not establish that the learned models are driven exclusively by Parkinsonian motor characteristics. However, several findings indicate that the motion signals contribute information beyond demographic correlations alone. First, the demographic-only models did not exceed 70% accuracy and showed poor minority-class recall, whereas the motion-based pipelines achieved stronger three-class performance. Second, the handcrafted model showed strong contributions from gyroscope-derived 3–7 Hz tremor descriptors, together with wavelet and broader frequency-domain features, which are physiologically plausible motion-derived descriptors associated with Parkinsonian motor characteristics rather than demographic variables. Third, the deep motion branch retained predictive value when combined with demographics, although its isolated contribution was substantially weaker than that of the demographic branch.
At the same time, the marked reduction in performance after removal of the demographic branch shows that the deep pipeline does not rely on motor information alone. The current results therefore support a mixed interpretation: the models capture clinically plausible movement characteristics, but also exploit demographic structure present in the PADS cohort. They cannot be assumed to separate these two sources of information completely.
The large performance reduction observed after removal of the demographic branch also indicates that the deep pipeline depends substantially on demographic information. Although this dependence improves prediction within the present cohort, it raises a generalisability concern: a model that relies strongly on age, weight, height, or related variables may perform differently in populations whose demographic distributions do not match those of the PADS dataset. The ablation result therefore demonstrates predictive dependence on demographics, but it does not establish demographic fairness or robustness across subgroups.
6.3. Handcrafted features versus learned embeddings
The comparison between the two pipelines reveals an informative asymmetry. The deep pipeline achieved higher recording-level accuracy than the handcrafted pipeline achieved at the segment level. This may partly reflect the longer 2048-sample input window and preservation of within-recording temporal structure, although the two lower-level units are not directly comparable. The handcrafted pipeline achieves better patient-level accuracy because probability aggregation benefits from a dense set of approximately 158 within-patient segment predictions on average, compared with multiple recording-level embeddings per patient, with substantially fewer prediction units than the approximately 158 handcrafted segments per patient.
Robustness should be interpreted here as evaluation-level stability rather than superior performance at every prediction scale. The deep pipeline was clearly stronger at the recording level, indicating better discrimination of individual recordings. In contrast, the handcrafted pipeline showed higher patient-level accuracy and more consistent performance after aggregating a dense set of within-patient predictions. Therefore, the handcrafted approach cannot be described as globally more robust; its advantage is specific to patient-level aggregation under the present protocol. A fair interpretation is therefore that the deep pipeline provides stronger individual-recording discrimination, whereas the handcrafted pipeline is more stable for aggregated patient-level decisions under the present protocol.
The higher patient-level performance of the handcrafted pipeline should not be interpreted as evidence that its feature representation is intrinsically superior to the learned deep embeddings. At their respective lower-level evaluation units, the deep pipeline achieved substantially higher recording-level accuracy than the handcrafted pipeline achieved at the segment level, indicating stronger per-recording discrimination. The patient-level advantage of the handcrafted pipeline is likely influenced by its denser segmentation scheme, which generates approximately 158 overlapping segment predictions per patient and therefore provides a larger set of probabilities for aggregation. This aggregation reduces within-patient noise and can improve the final patient-level decision even when individual segment predictions are weaker. Accordingly, the observed advantage is best interpreted as a property of the complete patient-level inference protocol, rather than of the handcrafted feature representation alone.
A definitive comparison of representation quality would require both pipelines to be evaluated under matched window lengths, matched numbers of predictions per patient, and the same aggregation procedure.
The ROC-AUC comparison provides an additional threshold-independent perspective on this difference. Although the deep pipeline achieved slightly stronger discrimination for healthy controls, the handcrafted ensemble achieved higher macro-average AUC and better discrimination of both Parkinson’s disease and Other Movement Disorders. The largest difference occurred for Other Movement Disorders, for which the handcrafted and deep pipelines achieved AUC values of 0.746 and 0.695, respectively. Therefore, the handcrafted pipeline’s patient-level advantage cannot be attributed solely to the final decision threshold, although differences in window length, prediction density, and aggregation strategy still prevent a definitive conclusion regarding intrinsic representation superiority.
The handcrafted pipeline provides stronger evidence of motor-specific learning because its highest-ranked variables included tremor-band, gyroscope-derived, wavelet, and broader frequency-domain descriptors rather than demographic attributes. Nevertheless, these importance rankings remain correlational and do not prove that the model’s decisions are independent of demographic confounding. For the deep pipeline, the learned 128-dimensional embeddings are less directly interpretable, and the substantial contribution of the demographic branch makes it difficult to isolate the proportion of performance attributable specifically to motor representation learning.
If the goal is to characterise individual movement episodes at high temporal resolution, the deep encoder is better suited because it preserves temporal structure within each segment. A combined system that uses deep embeddings for episode-level characterisation and handcrafted features for patient-level aggregation may offer the best of both approaches, and represents a natural direction for future work.
The cross-validation results indicate stable internal performance across patient-level folds within the same dataset, with a standard deviation of 3.19 percentage points in patient-level accuracy for the ensemble.
The handcrafted feature ablation further supports this interpretation. Removing cross-axis descriptors simplified the model from 160 to 150 features without loss of performance, while tremor-specific and frequency-domain descriptors remained the main contributors (Section 5.7).
6.4. Computational trade-offs
The computational analysis (Section 5.10) reveals a trade-off between predictive performance and implementation efficiency. Although the handcrafted ensemble achieved numerically higher patient-level performance, its end-to-end latency was greater because each recording was divided into overlapping segments and processed through explicit extraction of 150 descriptors. In contrast, the deep pipeline required a slightly larger serialised model but produced embeddings directly from each recording and achieved substantially lower recording-level latency.
The measured latencies suggest that both pipelines are computationally feasible for workstation or server-assisted screening. However, they do not demonstrate direct smartwatch deployment. On-device performance would depend on processor architecture, memory constraints, TensorFlow or tree-model runtime support, power consumption, sensor-stream handling, and model optimisation techniques such as quantisation or pruning.
6.5. The other movement disorders problem
The modest macro F1-scores reported throughout this study indicate that increasing architectural complexity or feature dimensionality cannot compensate for limited class separability, minority-class size, and single-modality information. In this study, simpler configurations selected through ablation were often more stable than more complex alternatives: the deep model without attention matched or exceeded the full attention-based variant, and the 150-feature handcrafted model matched or exceeded the 160-feature version, while the handcrafted ensemble as a whole achieved numerically higher patient-level performance than the deeper two-stage framework. This pattern suggests that the main performance bottleneck lies in the data and task formulation, namely the three-class problem, the imbalanced and clinically confusable Other Movement Disorders group, and reliance on a single wrist-motion modality, rather than in insufficient model complexity or experimentation.
This group was the hardest to classify at every stage and with every model. This difficulty can be attributed to both methodological and clinical factors. Part of the difficulty is attributable to the smaller and clinically heterogeneous Other Movement Disorders group. Although SMOTE was used in earlier auxiliary experiments, the final handcrafted pipeline relied on class-weighted training rather than synthetic oversampling. The remaining difficulty is also attributable to clinical reality. Essential tremor, atypical parkinsonism, and drug-induced movement disorders present with motor features that resemble Parkinson’s disease on a smartwatch, and these presentations can also be difficult for specialists to distinguish. Expecting a model trained on a single session of wrist movements to reliably separate them may therefore be an unrealistic expectation at this stage.
From a clinical perspective, the principal limitation is not the aggregate accuracy but the asymmetric class-wise error profile. Although Parkinson’s disease sensitivity was relatively high, its one-vs-rest specificity remained moderate, indicating that a meaningful proportion of healthy controls and patients with other movement disorders were incorrectly assigned to the Parkinson’s disease class. More importantly, sensitivity for Other Movement Disorders remained inadequate, meaning that the model failed to identify a substantial proportion of the most clinically confusable cases. Therefore, the reported accuracy improvement should not be interpreted as evidence of reliable differential screening performance.
Accordingly, we do not describe the achieved accuracy as clinically meaningful in itself. The clinical relevance of the framework refers only to its patient-level evaluation and inclusion of diagnostically confusable groups, whereas the current class-wise performance remains insufficient for stand-alone differential decision-making.
The practical implication is that the system as it stands is more useful as a PD screening tool than as a differential diagnosis tool. It identifies PD cases with reasonably high sensitivity and identifies healthy individuals reasonably well, but it should not be trusted to distinguish PD from other movement disorders without additional clinical input.
The auxiliary binary experiments were useful for decomposing the three-class problem into individual decision boundaries. Their results showed that some pairwise distinctions, particularly Parkinson’s disease versus non-PD, could achieve higher aggregate performance, while the Parkinson’s disease versus Other Movement Disorders boundary remained difficult. These experiments therefore served as diagnostic analyses of class separability rather than as the principal evidence for the proposed framework. All main conclusions regarding screening performance are based on the unified three-class patient-level evaluation.
6.5.1. Nested threshold evaluation for other movement disorders
To determine whether the limited recall of the Other Movement Disorders class was caused by the default argmax decision rule, we conducted a nested threshold evaluation. Within each outer patient-level fold, the training patients were further divided into inner training and validation subsets. The threshold for the Other Movement Disorders probability was selected exclusively on the inner validation subset and was then applied to the untouched outer test patients. This procedure prevented the outer test data from influencing threshold selection. The results are reported in Table 22.
The baseline values above were recomputed within the nested-threshold experimental pipeline and therefore differ slightly from the authoritative final five-fold values reported in Table 12 (patient accuracy 0.7228 ± 0.0319), because the additional inner-validation split was introduced specifically for threshold selection and was not part of the primary five-fold evaluation protocol.

