6.5.2. What the SHAP and failure analyses reveal
The SHAP and failure-case analyses provide a more detailed explanation of why the Other Movement Disorders class remained difficult. At the population level, Parkinson’s disease and Other Movement Disorders shared several influential frequency-band and tremor-power descriptors. This overlap is consistent with the fact that both groups contain patients with tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and related movement abnormalities, meaning that wrist-motion signals alone may not provide a clean diagnostic boundary.
At the individual-patient level, two qualitatively different error patterns were observed. Some patients were located close to the ensemble decision boundary, with nearly equal probabilities assigned to Parkinson’s disease and Other Movement Disorders. Such cases reflect model uncertainty and potentially genuine clinical ambiguity. Other patients were misclassified with comparatively large confidence margins. These cases exhibited feature combinations that strongly resembled the model’s learned Parkinson’s disease profile, despite belonging to an alternative movement-disorder category.
This distinction is important for potential screening use. Low-margin cases could potentially be flagged for manual review or additional testing, whereas high-confidence errors cannot be resolved through simple confidence thresholding alone. The nested threshold experiment supports this conclusion, as threshold optimisation did not improve Other Movement Disorders recall. Improving these cases will likely require additional modalities, more detailed diagnostic subclass labels, medication-state information, or longitudinal observations rather than a different output threshold alone.
The SHAP results also refine the interpretation of the conventional feature-importance ranking. While tremor-band and gyroscope-derived descriptors remained influential, the global patient-level analysis identified substantial contributions from wavelet and broader frequency-band features. Therefore, the classifier’s behaviour cannot be reduced to a single tremor-power variable; it relies on a distributed representation of spectral content, signal variability, and multi-scale temporal structure.
6.6. Recording-level versus patient-level results
For the final deep pipeline under five-fold cross-validation, patient-level aggregation produced a modest increase over recording-level performance, from 0.683 ± 0.040 to 0.689 ± 0.046 in accuracy. This limited change indicates that predictions were generally consistent across recordings from the same subject, although aggregation provided a small net benefit. A model that produced inconsistent recording-level predictions would have shown a larger shift after aggregation. This consistency is an encouraging indication of within-subject consistency under the controlled evaluation setting, in which a clinician would require a consistent classification across multiple measurement sessions before acting on it.
For the handcrafted pipeline, patient-level aggregation substantially improved performance relative to segment-level evaluation. This indicates that averaging a dense set of temporally correlated segment probabilities can reduce within-patient prediction noise more strongly than the aggregation observed across the smaller number of recording-level predictions in the deep pipeline. In the handcrafted pipeline, patient-level aggregation combined many temporally correlated segment predictions. This reduced within-patient prediction noise, but the resulting gain should be interpreted as an aggregation effect rather than as evidence from independent observations.
The corrected overlap-sensitivity analysis showed that removing overlap reduced patient-level accuracy from 72.28% to 70.58%, while macro F1 decreased from 0.6623 to 0.6284. This confirms that overlap contributed to the effectiveness of patient-level probability aggregation, especially for class-balanced performance. However, the persistence of approximately 70.6% patient-level accuracy with non-overlapping segments indicates that the result was not produced solely by repeated overlapping observations. Moreover, segment-level accuracy slightly increased when overlap was removed, demonstrating that overlap did not uniformly inflate performance at every evaluation level.
Therefore, the handcrafted pipeline’s patient-level performance should be interpreted as the combined result of the feature representation, dense within-patient sampling, and probability aggregation, rather than as evidence from statistically independent segment observations.
6.7. Limitations
Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the present findings. First, although both pipelines were evaluated using five-fold patient-level cross-validation, they were not compared under fully matched segmentation, aggregation, and model-selection protocols. The handcrafted pipeline used shorter overlapping windows and denser patient-level aggregation, whereas the deep pipeline operated on longer recording-level inputs with patient-wise inner validation and early stopping. Therefore, the observed patient-level differences reflect the complete inference protocols rather than feature representation alone. In addition, the XGBoost–LightGBM ensemble weights were fixed heuristically at 60:40 rather than optimised through fully nested validation, and the deep architecture was not subjected to an exhaustive hyperparameter search.
Second, all experiments were conducted on the PADS dataset, which was collected using a single smartwatch platform during predefined motor tasks under controlled conditions. The study did not evaluate free-living activities, longitudinal disease progression, medication-state variation, sensor-placement changes, device heterogeneity, interrupted signals, environmental vibration, or persistent motion artefacts. No dedicated signal-quality, artefact-detection, or denoising module was included. Accordingly, the present results demonstrate controlled-condition screening feasibility only and should not be interpreted as evidence of real-world or on-device deployment readiness.
Third, potential demographic confounding was not fully resolved. Age, age at diagnosis, weight, and height were influential in several experiments, and the demographic branch contributed substantially to the deep pipeline. The models may therefore partly exploit cohort-specific demographic differences between diagnostic groups. Performance was not assessed separately across demographic subgroups, and comparable sensitivity, specificity, macro F1-score, ROC-AUC, or calibration across age, sex, and body-size strata cannot be assumed.
Fourth, the explainability analysis remains model-specific. SHAP values were calculated only for the XGBoost component of the soft-voting ensemble and therefore do not fully decompose the combined XGBoost–LightGBM decision. Moreover, SHAP values describe predictive associations rather than causal neurological mechanisms. Correlated spectral and tremor features may share attribution, and averaging explanations across recordings may conceal task-specific variation.
Fifth, overlapping segmentation introduced temporal dependence and pseudo-replication within recordings. Patient-level partitioning prevented cross-subject leakage, and principal conclusions were based on patient-level test units; however, the overlap-sensitivity analysis did not fully separate the effect of overlap from the effect of prediction density. Similarly, the robustness of patient-level aggregation to different numbers of recordings per participant was not directly evaluated. The CNN–LSTM multi-seed analysis was also a convergence diagnostic rather than an independent performance estimate because the held-out set was used for early-stopping monitoring.
Finally, the study was not designed as an exhaustive benchmark of modern temporal learning methods. Transformer-based, temporal convolutional, and self-supervised approaches were not evaluated. The dataset also lacked structured longitudinal follow-up, session-matched MDS-UPDRS scores, medication-state annotations, and independent blinded neurologist predictions. Consequently, the handcrafted descriptors should be interpreted as clinically motivated predictive features rather than validated neurological biomarkers. Future work should include prospective multi-site validation, free-living recordings, multiple wearable devices, subgroup and calibration analyses, controlled artefact and dropout experiments, demographic-matched evaluation, clinician–algorithm comparison, and matched protocols for comparing modern temporal models.
7. Conclusion
This work compared two complementary approaches to automated three-class Parkinson’s disease screening from smartwatch data. The final No-attention CNN-demographic deep pipeline achieved a five-fold patient-level accuracy of 68.9%±4.6%
, a macro F1-score of 0.618 ± 0.042, and a macro ROC-AUC of 0.799 across dataset-wide out-of-fold predictions for all 469 subjects. The handcrafted ensemble achieved 72.28%±3.19%
 patient-level accuracy, a macro F1-score of 0.662 ± 0.037, and a macro ROC-AUC of 0.825 under the corresponding patient-level cross-validation protocol. The handcrafted ensemble was numerically higher on all three metrics, but paired statistical testing (exact McNemar test for accuracy; paired bootstrap for macro F1 and macro ROC-AUC) did not identify these differences as statistically significant at 𝛼=0.05
. However, this aggregate performance masks clinically important class-specific limitations. Parkinson’s disease sensitivity was comparatively strong, whereas its specificity remained moderate, and sensitivity for Other Movement Disorders remained inadequate. Therefore, the reported accuracy should be interpreted as evidence of preliminary screening utility rather than sufficient differential classification performance. The findings indicate that the models exploit both motor and demographic information; therefore, the reported performance should not be interpreted as evidence that the learned representations are independent of demographic confounding.
Complementary conventional importance and out-of-fold patient-level SHAP analyses showed that the handcrafted model relied on a distributed combination of wavelet, frequency-band, tremor-power, and time-domain variability descriptors. The explainability analysis therefore provided clinically plausible feature-level evidence while also showing that no single descriptor fully determined the model output. Ablation analysis showed that cross-axis handcrafted descriptors and the deep attention block did not provide consistent patient-level gains, leading to simpler final models with clearer interpretation.
The principal unresolved limitation was the separation of Parkinson’s disease from other movement disorders. That boundary was hard to draw at every stage. The limited sample size and clinical heterogeneity of the Other Movement Disorders class likely contributed to its poor sensitivity. Enlarging and better characterising this group may provide greater benefit than increasing model complexity alone.
Patient-level failure analysis showed that the principal errors included both low-margin cases located close to the Parkinson’s disease–Other Movement Disorders boundary and high-confidence errors in which alternative movement-disorder patients exhibited strongly Parkinson-like motion profiles. These findings reinforce that the remaining limitation is not simply an inappropriate classification threshold, but incomplete separability of clinically overlapping disorders using wrist-motion data alone.
Accordingly, the primary contribution of this study is not a new CNN, gradient-boosting algorithm, or ensemble rule, but a validation-oriented framework for comparing established modelling strategies under a unified three-class, patient-level evaluation with out-of-fold prediction, statistical comparison, and detailed analysis of clinically important failure modes.
The two-stage design and the handcrafted pipeline each offer distinct advantages. A combined system that uses deep embeddings for episode-level characterisation and handcrafted features for patient-level aggregation may offer the best of both approaches, and represents a natural direction for future work alongside validation on multi-site datasets and alternative wearable platforms. Therefore, the present findings support research-stage screening feasibility under controlled conditions, not immediate clinical or free-living deployment.
